      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{63691297469f06772579fe6f|full_name} Well, _supporting_ every Unicode character - not so hard, just stop blocking them. I've worked for a company as a developer in a context where we had to support internationalised text of all kinds before - it isn't impossible, it's just hard. Competent developers can do it. Do VRChat hire any?\n\n_Rendering_ every Unicode character, somewhat harder.\n\nNote that \"supporting\" and \"rendering\" are not the same thing. If you show the character as a square box, that can be considered supporting it correctly. Solving the problem of having a font which contains the character is a next step thing. Not showing the character at all, or filtering it out, is just incorrect behaviour.\n\nNonetheless it's a character I have in all my bios everywhere else, but VRChat is unnecessarily blocking it. Whether you personally consider it worth using or not is sort of irrelevant - it's already in use, by me, right now, pretty everywhere other than VRChat.\n\nAnd no, \"security reasons\" has never been, and will never be, an acceptable reason for having bad internationalisation in an application.\n\nAt least you have done the research, though. Turned digit 3 (↋) is indeed used to mean \"eleven\" in dozenal circles. That is exactly the meaning I have used it for in \"G↋n\" - the digit eleven stands in for omitting the middle eleven digits of \"Globalisation\".",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
